{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion lag: The model fails to anticipate sharp downward corrections (e.g., 89 to 77) and instead projects a slow, linear drift. Apply an immediate downward adjustment of 5-10% when the 18-day history shows a peak followed by a decline, regardless of flat sentiment.",
    "Adjust for delayed upward spikes: The model consistently underestimates the magnitude and speed of recovery rallies (e.g., 77 to 95). When the true outcome shows a V-shaped recovery, increase the forecast slope by 20-30% for the first 10 days of the horizon.",
    "Ignore low sentiment signals during volatility: Sentiment history remains near zero (0.00) during major price swings (both drops and rallies), indicating the sentiment feature is uninformative for this specific volatility regime. Do not rely on sentiment to trigger directional changes; instead, use the momentum of the last 3-5 days of price history.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon bias: The model exhibits a 'flat-line' bias, predicting a near-constant price regardless of the 30-day horizon. Replace the linear projection with a non-linear curve that captures the high volatility amplitude observed in the true outcomes (range of ~18 EUR).",
    "Overshoot correction: After a sharp drop, the model's recovery is too gradual. If the price drops below the 20-day moving average, force a steeper upward trajectory in the forecast to match the historical recovery speed seen in the data."
  ]
}
